+### 1. How does a page.jsonl page route its children?
+
+**a — fork the checkpoint** (score 3; 12.7s, $0.034)
+
+A `page.jsonl` page routes its children through the same `Page.child(name)` in `Page.class.js`, but a `page.jsonl` file line short-circuits the ordinary filesystem probe.
+
+Here's the chain of reasoning:
+
+1. **Declaring the link.** `Log.js`'s `file(name)` method (called by `set()` for every `{"file": "…"}` line) tests the name against `CHILD` — the regex `/^([^/]+)\/page\.(jsonl|js)$/`. A line like `{"file": "kid/page.jsonl"}` matches, so it calls `link_child(name)`, which in turn calls `child_file(entry)`. That records `this.child_kinds.set("kid", "jsonl")` (a Map on the page) and adds `"kid"` to `this.children` as an unresolved entry (`children.set(dir, null)`) if it isn't already there.
+
+2. **Resolving the link at navigation time.** When the Router walks a url and calls `page.child("kid")` (`Page.class.js`), the ordinary order is: memory (`known = this.children.get(name)`), then `route()`, then a `page.js` probe (`Page.load`), then a `.md` fallback. But before the `route()`/probe steps, `child()` has this specific check:
+
+```js
+if (this.child_kinds?.get(name) === "jsonl") {
+    const page = await Page.jsonl(this.url + name + "/");
+    return page && this.add(name, page).load_all_children(levels);
+}
+```
+
+So a child that was declared via a `kid/page.jsonl` file line is read straight through `Page.jsonl(url)` (in `Log.js`) — **no import probe, no guessing**. This is the "no probe" case doc/declaring.md and the jsonl doc call out explicitly: normal folders are discovered by trying `Page.load()` (a dynamic `import()`) and falling through to `.md`; a `page.jsonl`-linked child skips straight to the log reader because the page already told you what kind of file answers that name.
+
+3. **What `Page.jsonl()` does.** It's a static on `PageLog` (`Log.js`): builds a `Reader` over `<url>page.jsonl`, awaits `reader.open()` (which streams live on localhost via `ext/JSONL/live.js`, or does a one-shot fetch off localhost), reads line 1 as the constructor args (optionally naming `"class"` to build a `Page` subclass instead of a plain `Page`/`Card`), sets `page.content ??= page.log_view`, and attaches the reader so later lines stream in via `set()`.
+
+One more wrinkle worth naming: a `page.js` parent can declare the same link by hand in its `children:` string — `children: "generator jsonl/page.jsonl"` — and `declare()` routes that string through `this.child_file(child)` too (`Page.class.js`, the `declare()` method calls `child_file` on each string entry), which populates `child_kinds` the same way the jsonl log's own `file()` line does. So both a `page.js` parent and a `page.jsonl` parent end up feeding the identical `child_kinds` Map that `child()` checks.
