+A page.jsonl doesn't route its own children with special logic — it uses the same `child_kinds` map every Page has, just populated a different way.
+
+Each `{"file": "kid/page.jsonl"}` line runs `set()` → `file()` (Log.js:50-53), which sees the name matches `CHILD` (`/^([^/]+)\/page\.(jsonl|js)$/`, Log.js:19) and calls `link_child()` (Log.js:74-77). `link_child` calls `child_file(name)` (Log.js:66-72), which records `this.child_kinds.set("kid", "jsonl")` and returns `"kid"`, then sets `this.children.set("kid", null)` — a declared-but-unresolved child, same shape a page.js's `declare()` produces for a plain string entry (Page.class.js:114).
